{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00326 - MONDO_1566",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Glucoheptonate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypercalcemia Disease through a mechanism involving phosphorus metabolism. The evidence suggests that Calcium Glucoheptonate is linked to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, which in turn is associated with two different compounds—Calcium Acetate and Calcium Chloride—both of which are indicated to cause Hypercalcemia Disease. This implies that the compound may influence phosphorus homeostasis, potentially leading to increased calcium levels in the blood. The presence of multiple pathways connecting Calcium Glucoheptonate to Hypercalcemia Disease highlights the complexity of the relationship, with the exact mechanism likely involving the regulation of phosphorus metabolism and its downstream effects on calcium levels. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of phosphorus in the development of hypercalcemia, suggesting that Calcium Glucoheptonate may act as a mediator in this process.",
